<div><p class="kick">Roast &middot; Portion &middot; Serve</p>
<h1>Dinner for other people, timed properly.</h1>
<p class="lead">Roastly is a home-cooking site about the part of dinner that goes wrong: how long things really take, how much to cook, and what order to do it in so everything arrives hot at once.</p>
<p class="hero-cta"><a class="btn" href="#tools">Open the tools</a> <a class="btn gh" href="roasting-guide.html">Read the roasting guide</a></p></div></div></section>
<div class="serve"><div class="wrap">
<div><b>8</b><span>cuts timed</span></div><div><b>12</b><span>portion figures</span></div><div><b>0</b><span>sponsored ingredients</span></div><div><b>Free</b><span>no sign-up</span></div></div></div>
<section><div class="wrap"><div class="sec-h"><p class="kick">How it goes wrong</p><h2>Three failures, one cause</h2><p>Cold vegetables, dry meat and a host who never sits down all come from the same thing: no written order of events.</p></div>
<div class="steps">
<div class="step"><h3>Start from the clock</h3><p>Decide when people eat, then work backwards. Everything else, including what you cook, follows from that one number.</p></div>
<div class="step"><h3>Trust a thermometer</h3><p>Minutes per kilogram gets you close. A probe in the thickest part tells you the truth, and costs less than one ruined joint.</p></div>
<div class="step"><h3>Rest, then make the gravy</h3><p>The twenty minutes the meat spends resting is exactly the twenty minutes you need for the sauce and the greens. That is not a coincidence.</p></div>
</div></div></section>
<section class="tools" id="tools"><div class="wrap">
<div class="sec-h"><p class="kick">Free tools</p><h2>Three things worked out for you</h2><p>Everything runs in your browser. Nothing is saved and nothing is sent anywhere.</p></div>
<div class="tool" id="roast"><h3>Roast Timer</h3><p>Pick a cut and a weight for oven temperature, cooking time, target centre temperature and when to take it out.</p>
<div class="tool-grid"><div><div class="fld"><label for="rCut">What are you roasting?</label><select id="rCut"><option value="chickenWhole">Whole chicken</option><option value="chickenPieces">Chicken pieces</option><option value="porkLoin">Pork loin</option><option value="lambLeg">Leg of lamb</option><option value="beefJoint">Beef joint</option><option value="turkeyCrown">Turkey crown</option><option value="fishWhole">Whole fish</option><option value="vegTray">Tray of vegetables</option></select></div>
<div class="fld"><label for="rKg">Weight (kg)</label><input id="rKg" type="number" min="0.2" max="12" step="0.1" value="1.8"></div>
<figure style="margin:0"><img src="https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1616401616927-3c81de22dfa8?auto=format&fit=crop&w=700&q=75" alt="Roasted chicken on a white ceramic plate" width="700" height="470" loading="lazy" style="aspect-ratio:3/2;object-fit:cover"></figure></div>
<div class="out" id="rOut"></div></div></div>
<div class="tool" id="portion"><h3>Portion Planner</h3><p>How much to buy and cook, so you are neither mean nor throwing half of it away.</p>
<div class="tool-grid"><div><div class="fgrid"><div class="fld"><label for="pGuests">People at the table</label><input id="pGuests" type="number" min="1" max="40" value="6"></div>
<div class="fld"><label for="pApp">Appetite</label><select id="pApp"><option value="0.8">Light</option><option value="1" selected>Normal</option><option value="1.2">Hearty</option><option value="1.4">Generous, with leftovers</option></select></div></div>
<div class="fld"><label for="pCourse">Course</label><select id="pCourse"><option value="starter">Starter</option><option value="main" selected>Main course</option><option value="pudding">Pudding</option><option value="drinks">Drinks</option></select></div>
<figure style="margin:0"><img src="https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1788529933261-06dda639e214?auto=format&fit=crop&w=700&q=75" alt="A wooden dining table set with white plates" width="700" height="470" loading="lazy" style="aspect-ratio:3/2;object-fit:cover"></figure></div>
<div class="out" id="pOut"></div></div></div>
<div class="tool" id="plan"><h3>Dinner Timeline</h3><p>Give us a serving time and we will write the order of events backwards from it.</p>
<div class="tool-grid"><div><div class="fgrid"><div class="fld"><label for="tServe">Sitting down at</label><input id="tServe" type="time" value="19:30"></div>
<div class="fld"><label for="tCourses">Courses</label><select id="tCourses"><option value="1">One</option><option value="2">Two</option><option value="3" selected>Three</option></select></div></div>
<div class="fld"><label for="tOven">Oven space</label><select id="tOven"><option value="one" selected>One oven</option><option value="two">Two ovens or an oven and a hob to spare</option></select></div>
<figure style="margin:0"><img src="https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1681249281123-323694ea0f98?auto=format&fit=crop&w=700&q=75" alt="A dining table with place settings" width="700" height="470" loading="lazy" style="aspect-ratio:3/2;object-fit:cover"></figure></div>
<div class="out" id="tOut"></div></div></div>
</div></section>
<section class="band"><img src="https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1771161409360-e07e34e20c1b?auto=format&fit=crop&w=1600&q=75" alt="" width="1600" height="900" loading="lazy">
<div class="wrap"><p class="kick">The honest version</p><h2>Nobody remembers the gravy. They remember whether you sat down.</h2>
<p>A host who spends the evening at the stove has cooked a worse dinner than one who served something simple and stayed at the table. Plan for that, not for a menu that needs four hands.</p>
<p style="margin-top:1.3em"><a class="btn" style="background:#D98E2B;color:#241B18" href="dinner-party-guide.html">Read the hosting guide</a></p></div></section>
<section><div class="wrap"><div class="sec-h"><p class="kick">Long reads</p><h2>Two guides</h2></div>
<div class="cards">
<article class="card"><img src="https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1670398564097-0762e1b30b3a?auto=format&fit=crop&w=800&q=75" alt="Sliced chicken breast with green peas" width="800" height="500" loading="lazy"><div class="cb"><p class="kick">Guide one</p><h3>The Roasting Guide</h3><p>Temperatures, times, resting, browning, gravy from the tray, and how to use a thermometer so you never guess again.</p><p><a class="btn gh" href="roasting-guide.html">Read the guide</a></p></div></article>
<article class="card"><img src="https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1688437307968-5147d11395c2?auto=format&fit=crop&w=800&q=75" alt="A table with a vase of flowers and candles" width="800" height="500" loading="lazy"><div class="cb"><p class="kick">Guide two</p><h3>The Hosting Guide</h3><p>Menus you can actually serve alone, what to make ahead, laying a table, pacing the evening and recovering when something goes wrong.</p><p><a class="btn gh" href="dinner-party-guide.html">Read the guide</a></p></div></article>
</div></div></section>
<section style="padding-top:0"><div class="wrap"><div class="sec-h"><p class="kick">Questions</p><h2>Asked every weekend</h2></div>
<div class="faq"><details><summary>How long should I roast a chicken?</summary><p>Roughly 40 minutes per kilogram at 200&deg;C, plus 20 minutes, then rest it for at least 15. But weight is only a guide: the reliable test is a thermometer in the thickest part of the thigh, away from the bone, reading 74&deg;C.</p></details><details><summary>Why does resting meat matter?</summary><p>During cooking the juices are driven towards the centre. Resting lets them redistribute, so they stay in the meat instead of running onto the board. Ten to twenty minutes loosely covered makes a visible difference, and large joints hold their heat far longer than people expect.</p></details><details><summary>How much food per person should I cook?</summary><p>For a main course, roughly 350 g of meat on the bone or 180 g boneless, 250 g potatoes and 200 g vegetables. Cook a little extra of the cheap things and exactly enough of the expensive ones; nobody has ever complained about too many roast potatoes.</p></details><details><summary>Should I use the fan setting?</summary><p>Fan ovens circulate air, so they cook faster and more evenly. The usual conversion is to drop the temperature by about 20&deg;C from a conventional recipe. For roasting, fan gives better browning; for delicate bakes, conventional is gentler.</p></details><details><summary>How do I get everything hot at the same time?</summary><p>Work backwards from when you want to sit down, and write it on paper. Start with the thing that takes longest and can wait, which is almost always the roast. Anything green goes last. The rest is making gravy while the meat rests.</p></details></div></div></section>
